An Open Letter to the American Government on Black American Mathematics Education

To the President of the United States, Congress, the U.S. Department of Education, Governors, State Legislatures, and American School Districts

Re: A National Mathematics Emergency and a New Strategy for Black American Students

Dear American Leaders,

America should be willing to have an uncomfortable but constructive conversation about mathematics education among Black American students.

The issue is not that Black children lack mathematical ability.

The issue is that far too many Black children are being educated in circumstances that do not consistently provide them with the opportunity, preparation, resources, expectations, and continuity necessary to reach their mathematical potential.

The federal government should therefore treat mathematics education as a national development priority—and Black American mathematics education as an area requiring particularly serious attention.

The latest National Assessment of Educational Progress (NAEP) results demonstrate that mathematics performance remains deeply unequal across student groups. At the same time, NAEP data show enormous variation within every racial group. Race does not determine mathematical ability, and averages should never be interpreted as statements about the capabilities of individual children. (Nation’s Report Card)

The appropriate question is therefore not:

“Why are Black students bad at mathematics?”

The appropriate question is:

“What conditions are preventing too many Black students from developing advanced mathematical competence—and what can America do about it?”

The Sincere Reasons for the Mathematics Gap

There is no single explanation.

Research points toward a combination of factors.

1. Unequal access to advanced mathematics

Black students are less likely in some educational settings to have access to high-level mathematics and science courses. This matters enormously.

Mathematics is cumulative.

A student who misses important concepts in fractions, proportional reasoning, algebra, geometry, or functions can encounter increasingly severe difficulties later.

By the time that student reaches high school calculus, the problem may appear to be “calculus ability” when the actual problem began years earlier.

The federal government should therefore measure mathematical opportunity, not merely mathematical outcomes.

Every student should have a measurable pathway:

Arithmetic → Pre-Algebra → Algebra I → Geometry → Algebra II → Precalculus → Calculus/Statistics/Advanced Mathematics

with multiple pathways into computer science, engineering, economics, physics, data science, and other quantitative fields.

2. Poverty and socioeconomic inequality

Mathematics does not exist independently of a child’s environment.

Housing instability, food insecurity, transportation problems, inadequate access to technology, family economic stress, and unequal access to tutoring can all affect the amount of time and cognitive energy available for learning.

NAEP’s own data show substantial differences in mathematics performance associated with socioeconomic status, although these associations should not be interpreted as proving a single causal mechanism. (Nation’s Report Card)

America should therefore stop treating mathematics remediation as merely a classroom problem.

Sometimes the mathematical problem is also an economic problem.

3. Unequal teacher experience and course availability

The Institute of Education Sciences reports that Black students are more likely in some contexts to attend schools with higher concentrations of inexperienced teachers and teachers who have not met all certification or licensure requirements. Research also identifies unequal access to advanced courses as an important educational opportunity issue. (Institute of Education Sciences)

This does not mean that Black teachers are required to teach Black students.

It means that every child deserves highly capable mathematics instruction.

America should create substantially stronger incentives for experienced mathematics teachers to work in schools serving historically underserved communities.

4. Educational segregation and concentrated disadvantage

American schools remain affected by patterns of residential and economic segregation.

Research from the National Center for Education Statistics has found that the Black-White mathematics achievement gap persists across different levels of Black student concentration, while differences within schools—including how resources and educational experiences are distributed—can be particularly important. (Institute of Education Sciences)

This should caution policymakers against searching for a simplistic explanation.

The problem is not simply “Black schools.”

Nor is it simply “White schools.”

The deeper question is whether students receive comparable opportunities to learn.

5. Expectations and academic culture

Students respond to the expectations communicated to them.

If mathematics is presented as something that only a small group of “naturally gifted” children can master, many students will conclude that mathematical ability is an innate trait rather than a skill that develops through disciplined practice.

Schools should replace this mentality with a culture of mathematical mastery:

You are capable of learning mathematics.
You are expected to learn mathematics.
We will provide the instruction necessary for you to learn mathematics.

Research on belonging and stereotype threat has also examined how negative stereotypes and students’ perceptions of belonging can affect academic performance. (American Psychological Association)

This does not mean lowering standards.

It means pairing high expectations with high support.

6. Mathematics learning gaps compound over time

One of America’s greatest mathematical problems is that educational systems frequently discover learning gaps too late.

A student can progress from grade to grade without mastering foundational concepts.

Eventually the accumulated deficit becomes enormous.

We should therefore build a national system of continuous mathematical diagnostics.

Not punitive testing.

Not labeling.

Diagnosis.

A student should be able to receive a mathematical profile showing:

* number sense;
* arithmetic fluency;
* fractions;
* ratios;
* algebraic reasoning;
* geometry;
* probability;
* statistics;
* functions;
* mathematical modeling;
* computational thinking.

The objective would be to identify precisely what the student knows and precisely what the student needs to learn next.

America Needs a Mathematics Reconstruction Program

I propose that the federal government establish a National Black Mathematics Excellence Initiative.

Its purpose would not be to segregate education.

Its purpose would be to ensure that Black American students have extraordinary access to mathematics.

The program should include:

1. Free national mathematics tutoring

Every student in an identified high-need school should have access to free after-school and summer mathematics instruction.

2. Mathematics academies

Create publicly funded Saturday and summer mathematics academies emphasizing:

* algebra;
* geometry;
* calculus;
* statistics;
* computer science;
* mathematical modeling;
* financial mathematics;
* physics;
* engineering.

3. Universal access to advanced courses

Every high school should have a pathway to advanced mathematics.

Where a school cannot economically provide a course, students should be allowed to take it through a neighboring school, community college, online program, or other accredited institution.

4. National mathematics mentorship

Connect Black students with Black mathematicians, engineers, physicists, economists, statisticians, computer scientists, actuaries, and other quantitative professionals.

Representation is not a substitute for instruction.

But seeing people who have successfully traveled a mathematical path can help make that path psychologically and socially imaginable.

5. Better mathematics teachers

Create major federal scholarships and loan-forgiveness programs for individuals entering mathematics teaching in underserved schools.

The goal should be simple:

Put excellent mathematics teachers wherever mathematical need is greatest.

6. Early mathematical education

The intervention should begin before middle school.

Children should develop strong foundations in:

counting → arithmetic → fractions → proportional reasoning → algebraic thinking.

A child who enters middle school with strong mathematical foundations has a dramatically different trajectory from a child who arrives already several years behind.

7. Mathematical culture

America should create a national culture in which mathematics is celebrated.

Mathematics competitions.

Mathematics festivals.

Public lectures.

Black mathematician biographies.

Mathematical games.

Engineering camps.

Coding programs.

Community mathematics laboratories.

Scholarships.

National mathematical challenges.

We should make mathematical excellence culturally prestigious.

We Should Measure the Right Things

The United States should publish a national Mathematical Opportunity Index.

Instead of merely asking:

“How did students score?”

we should also ask:

“What mathematical opportunities did students receive?”

The index could measure:

Course access + teacher qualifications + instructional time + tutoring access + technology + advanced-course availability + student mastery + postsecondary mathematics participation.

This would allow policymakers to distinguish between an achievement problem and an opportunity problem.

Do Not Lower the Standard

There is one principle that must remain absolutely clear:

Closing the mathematics gap cannot mean lowering mathematical standards.

Black students deserve access to the highest levels of mathematics.

They deserve calculus.

They deserve linear algebra.

They deserve differential equations.

They deserve statistics.

They deserve discrete mathematics.

They deserve computer science.

They deserve mathematical economics.

They deserve engineering.

And they deserve to encounter these subjects with the preparation necessary to succeed.

The objective should not be merely to increase test scores.

The objective should be to dramatically increase the number of Black Americans who become:

mathematicians, engineers, scientists, programmers, economists, actuaries, statisticians, physicians, researchers, entrepreneurs, professors, inventors, and technological leaders.

A Mathematical Futurology of Education

America should begin thinking about mathematics education as an intergenerational investment.

If one generation develops stronger mathematical competence, that generation can produce teachers, engineers, entrepreneurs, researchers, parents, mentors, and institutions capable of improving the next generation.

This creates a positive feedback loop:

Better mathematics education
→ more mathematical competence
→ more STEM participation
→ higher technological capacity
→ greater economic opportunity
→ more mathematical role models
→ stronger educational culture
→ better outcomes for the next generation.

This is not simply education policy.

It is national human-capital policy.

The Government Should Set an Ambitious Goal

I therefore call upon the American government to establish a national objective:

Every Black American child should have the opportunity to reach advanced mathematical proficiency regardless of ZIP code, family income, or school district.

That means measuring the problem honestly.

Identifying where opportunity is being lost.

Investing where the deficits are greatest.

Maintaining rigorous standards.

Recruiting excellent teachers.

Expanding tutoring.

Expanding advanced mathematics.

And refusing to accept the assumption that persistent disparities are inevitable.

Black American children are not a mathematical problem for America to explain away.

They are an enormous reservoir of human potential for America to develop.

The question before the United States is not whether Black students can become mathematically excellent.

History already gives us abundant evidence that they can.

The question is whether the United States will construct an education system capable of giving millions more children the opportunity to do so.

The answer should be yes.

Sincerely,

A Citizen Calling for American Mathematical Excellence
